---
term: Intervention
aka:
  - Mode of intervention
  - Modes of intervention
section: evolution-and-protection
order: 90
definition: An action that a party takes against a charter that harms its members or others.
seeAlso:
  - exit
  - secession
  - arbitration
---
The framework doesn't prescribe protections that every charter must contain. What protects a charter's parties depends too much on its circumstances, such as a war, an economic crisis, or social unrest. The framework names the modes of intervention instead.

Intervention follows the same two conflict modes as any other conflict. Parties can always act against a charter by struggle. A charter's articles, or a parent's, only decide which interventions are arbitrated and on what terms. Without arbitration, each mode becomes struggle: exit becomes desertion, secession becomes a war of independence, and demotion becomes sanctions.

The following table lists the modes:

| Mode | Who acts | Arbitrated form |
|---|---|---|
| [[Exit]] | A member | Withdrawal under the exit procedure |
| [[Secession]] | A subsidiary or a faction | Withdrawal with a share of the commons |
| [[Escalation]] | A member or subsidiary | Arbitration by the level above |
| [[Audit]] | The parent or a commission | Review of execution against the articles |
| [[Demotion]] | The parent | Moving the charter from member back to associate |
| [[Receivership]] | The parent | Temporary control of the charter's executive functions |
| [[Dissolution]] | The members or the parent | Ending the charter under its dissolution procedure |
| Reclassification | A peer or the parent | Changing the charter's [[status]] to adversary or enemy |
